+++ b/docs/en/administration/budgeted-reclamation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,103 @@
+# Experimental budgeted cold-chunk reclamation
+
+This candidate is based on v1.4.1. Enable it on one designated maintenance
+client using `--reclaim-interval`. Its default is zero (disabled). Read-only,
+`--no-bgjob`, and `--max-deletes 0` clients do not start the worker.
+
+## Recovery contract
+
+Ordinary compaction can be skipped when the client's compactor is busy. Without
+another IO trigger, historical references can remain in a cold chunk. The worker
+rediscovers candidates from existing durable chunk metadata. It does not rely
+on the process that originally missed a trigger surviving.
+
+One step processes due candidates, then reads a limited metadata page. It keeps
+the discovery cursor and a bounded observation queue between steps. A chunk must
+have matching complete raw-history fingerprints in observations separated by at
+least `--reclaim-min-age`. The check is repeated inside the compactor's admission
+guard, before allocating a new slice or invoking object compaction.
+
+The next step starts after the previous step finishes and the interval elapses
+(with jitter). This is not a fixed-rate overlapping timer. Failure and CAS-wasted
+attempts consume the same admission budget as successful attempts. Busy/failed
+candidates get bounded, backoff-delayed retries; unchanged metadata remains
+discoverable after those retries or after a client restart. Changed candidates
+leave the queue so continually changing chunks cannot monopolize it.
+
+Recovery is conditional on a surviving enabled client, eventually successful
+metadata/storage operations, and a stable candidate that meets the cost policy.
+The worker intentionally does not promise to reduce every chunk to one slice.
+
+## Cost policy
+
+The exact existing ordinary-compaction batch is planned (including `skipSome`
+and `maxCompactSlices`). Its new slice size is the logical rewrite cost.
+Estimated saving is the unique source slice sizes removed by that batch, minus
+the new slice size. Sources still referenced by an unprocessed tail or skipped
+prefix are excluded. Duplicate source IDs are counted once. Zero/negative saving
+plans, including sparse-only internal-hole expansion, are consequently rejected.
+
+Both an absolute minimum saving and a saving/rewrite ratio must be met. With the
+defaults, overwriting 256 KiB of a 64 MiB slice will not trigger a 64 MiB rewrite.
+Large-enough partial overwrites and high-gain short histories are still eligible
+after the stable observation interval.
+
+The saving is an estimate: cross-file cloned/shared slices and trash retention
+can lower or delay actual reclamation. Hard-linked files and paths deeper than
+the bounded 64-level ancestry check are conservatively excluded.
+
+## Options
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `--reclaim-interval` | `0` | Delay between steps; positive values enable the worker (minimum 1s) |
+| `--reclaim-min-age` | `10m` | Minimum separation of matching raw-history observations |
+| `--reclaim-scan-limit` | `128` | Discovery page and pending queue limit (maximum 4096) |
+| `--reclaim-max-chunks` | `1` | Maximum object-compaction attempts per step |
+| `--reclaim-max-bytes` | `64M` | Maximum admitted logical output bytes per step |
+| `--reclaim-min-bytes` | `64K` | Minimum estimated saving per batch |
+| `--reclaim-min-gain` | `25` | Minimum saving/output ratio in percent |
+
+A byte budget smaller than a candidate's output size excludes that candidate.
+The default 64 MiB can admit one complete chunk. The scan limit bounds scanned
+records and candidate counts, not every backend RPC or response byte. Ancestry
+and raw-history reads are additional bounded-per-candidate operations. Redis
+SCAN COUNT is a work hint and can return excess keys; those keys are consumed in
+later pages. KV enumeration includes non-chunk inode/dentry records as well.
+
+## Scope and operation
+
+`--subdir` limits compaction eligibility by checking ancestry at observation and
+execution. Metadata enumeration itself is a paginated volume scan, not a subtree
+index. SQL uses `(inode, indx)` keyset pagination; KV uses its ordered metadata
+keys; Redis scans the primary nodes and processes bounded key batches. Sparse
+files do not cause one read for every logical hole. No schema or dump format
+change is introduced, and no read transaction stays open during object rewriting.
+
+Use the maintenance mount's `--upload-limit`/`--download-limit` (Mbps) and upload/
+download concurrency to control actual storage traffic. Logical rewrite bytes
+are not an exact physical or network budget: block reads, compression, storage
+retries and deletion are separate costs. Reserve space for newly written objects
+until old references and objects can be deleted.
+
+Step logs report scanned/observed/checked counts, changed/rejected/deferred work,
+attempts, successes, failures, queue size, admitted logical bytes and estimated
+saving from successful commits. Estimated saving is not a measured capacity drop.
+
+## Boundaries still requiring deployment validation
+
+- Only one designated reclaimer per volume. This candidate has no distributed
+  lease or volume-wide resource budget. Ordinary compactors in other clients may
+  race; existing transactional origin checks still protect replacement.
+- Matching observations are a conservative stability heuristic, not a lock
+  preventing future writes. Concurrent appended writes are preserved by the
+  existing prefix transaction. Namespace changes can also race scope checks.
+- The cursor and observation ages are in memory. Restart begins discovery and
+  the cold observation interval again; it does not lose the durable source data.
+- Cancellation stops new admission and metadata discovery. An in-flight object
+  callback or existing context-free memory wait may still delay shutdown. The
+  logical per-step budget is not a strict wall-clock timeout.
+- Real TiKV/Ceph foreground latency, large-volume scan cost, Redis cluster
+  topology changes, and multi-client operation need deployment-specific tests.
+
+The implementation is an opt-in candidate, not a production acceptance result.
